 2. Get ready for rate hike(s) 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Fed governor Christopher Waller. Photo: Prakash Singh/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The elevated inflation reading all but locks in a Fed interest rate hike in December.

 Now, the debate is shifting to whether the Fed should entertain a super-sized rate hike in December — and how much further the central bank may need to tighten policy in 2027. State of play: Even the Federal Reserve officials who have argued for patience in getting inflation back under control are alarmed at the ongoing, broad-based price pressures of the sort evident in this morning's numbers.

 The two-year Treasury yield, which is sensitive to expectations of Fed policy, is up a stunning 0.20 percentage points this morning, indicating that traders see a rising chance the Fed will raise rates more than the one or two more times markets had expected. Treasury prices broadly fell, driving the yield on the 10-year Treasury note, a benchmark for mortgages and other loans, up above 5%. The intrigue: Before the CPI release, governor Christopher Waller delivered a speech in which he raised the possibility of a super-sized rate hike, not the quarter-point move that is the norm when the Fed is on a non-emergency footing.

 "To recap my economic outlook, economic activity is strong, the labor market is stable, but inflation is too high," Waller said in London. "This means that even if, as I expect, the monthly rate of inflation improves in October, I am leaning toward supporting an increase in our policy rate of 25 basis points" at a policy meeting that concludes Dec. 9. Yes, but: "That's my plan if things proceed as I expect," he continued — before saying that if the October CPI data turned out to be like the last couple of months, with core inflation at 0.3% or higher, "then a stronger policy move may be needed."

 "In that case, I believe a 50 basis point hike in the policy rate should be considered at the December meeting," Waller said. "Hiking only 25 basis points would put our credibility at risk, a risk I am not eager to take." Between the lines: That is exactly the report — with 0.3% core inflation — that arrived mere hours after Waller's comments.

 Add in two current voters on the Fed policy committee who dissented at the last meeting, instead favoring a hike (Lisa Cook and Beth Hammack), and other voters who have leaned hawkish lately (Michael Barr, Lorie Logan, Neel Kashkari), and there is a significant contingent that is worried the Fed's policy is incorrectly calibrated. The double whammy of Waller's comments and the inflation report drove market-based odds of a half-point rate hike in December to 14% this morning, per CME's FedWatch tool . Those chances were nil yesterday. Of note: Logan, the president of the Dallas Fed, delivered a speech this morning post-CPI report in which she made clear she favors two more interest rate increases — and even seemed open to the extra-large rate hike Waller discussed.

 ""I currently estimate that the Fed funds target range is at least 50 basis points — half a percentage point — below the level that would create some modest restriction and begin to bring the inflation trend back to target," Logan said at an energy conference in Houston. "The exact pace of increases could depend on the near-term economic and financial environment. It may be possible to move more rapidly this winter than the FOMC has moved so far this fall," Logan said. The bottom line: The tightening shall continue until morale (or at least core CPI inflation) improves.

 
 
